WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:05.996
Long column names like zero point eleven, sex of household head, are hard to

2
00:00:05.996 --> 00:00:08.223
type and easy to get wrong.

3
00:00:08.223 --> 00:00:13.791
In this lesson, you will learn two ways to give your columns short, clear names.

4
00:00:13.791 --> 00:00:16.760
Let's make our table easier to work with.

5
00:00:18.700 --> 00:00:24.215
Under the note, to rename a column name, the first way uses the names function.

6
00:00:24.215 --> 00:00:29.362
Names of demographic, arrow, c, and then the new names in quotes, in the

7
00:00:29.362 --> 00:00:31.200
same order as the columns.

8
00:00:33.200 --> 00:00:38.643
Province, District, Gender, Marital underscore Status, and H H underscore Size.

9
00:00:38.643 --> 00:00:41.612
We use underscores instead of spaces.

10
00:00:41.612 --> 00:00:46.560
Names without spaces are much easier to use in R.

11
00:00:51.200 --> 00:00:53.634
Run it, and open the table.

12
00:00:53.634 --> 00:00:55.257
Look at the headers:

13
00:00:55.257 --> 00:00:59.720
Province, District, Gender, Marital Status, H H Size. Clean and short!

14
00:01:08.200 --> 00:01:09.203
But be careful:

15
00:01:09.203 --> 00:01:11.542
names replaces all the names in order.

16
00:01:11.542 --> 00:01:16.220
If you get the order wrong, the names will be on the wrong columns.

17
00:01:33.200 --> 00:01:37.880
So here is the second way, which I often prefer. I write: Or.

18
00:01:37.880 --> 00:01:42.920
Then I recreate the table, so we start again from the original long names.

19
00:01:58.200 --> 00:01:59.390
Demographic, pipe, rename.

20
00:01:59.390 --> 00:02:05.340
Inside rename, we write the new name, an equals sign, and then the old name.

21
00:02:05.340 --> 00:02:07.720
New name first, old name second.

22
00:02:28.200 --> 00:02:31.483
Province equals zero point one province name.

23
00:02:31.483 --> 00:02:34.766
District equals zero point two district name.

24
00:02:34.766 --> 00:02:37.580
Gender equals sex of household head.

25
00:02:48.200 --> 00:02:51.705
Marital status, and household size, the same way.

26
00:02:51.705 --> 00:02:57.840
IntelliSense helps me pick each column, so the old names are spelled exactly right.

27
00:03:18.200 --> 00:03:22.663
Rename only touches the columns you list, so it is safer.

28
00:03:22.663 --> 00:03:26.720
And the code tells everyone exactly what changed. Excellent work!

